---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

import css from '../styles/pages/index.module.css';
import grid from '../styles/grid.module.css';

import dmjx from '../images/dmjx.png';
import lassekorsgaard from '../images/lassekorsgaard.jpg';
import nielsotto from '../images/nielsotto.jpg';
import ninafranziskachristiansen from '../images/ninafranziskachristiansen.jpg';
import patrikhuebner from '../images/patrikhuebner.jpg';
import paulnicholas from '../images/paulnicholas.jpg';
import pernilleeva1 from '../images/pernilleeva1.jpg';
import pernilleeva2 from '../images/pernilleeva2.png';
---

<Layout
  title="Processing Community Day Copenhagen 2026"
  year="2026"
  class={css.root}
>
  <div class={css.header}>
    <div class:list={[grid.container, grid.grid]}>
      <div class:list={[grid.col, css.menu]}>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="#speakers">Speakers</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#about">About</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#contact">Contact</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class:list={[grid.col, css.title]}>
        <h1>Processing Community Day CPH</h1>
      </div>
      <div class:list={[grid.col, css.info]}>
        <h3>When?</h3>
        <p>
          Saturday October 24th, 2026
          <br />
          11:00 am
          <br />
          Afterparty at{' '}
          <a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/QdaYCxa1zWh6BjtR7">Åben Brewery</a>
        </p>
        <h3>Where?</h3>
        <p>
          Danish School of Media and Journalism
          <br />
          <a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/gVg27hSGXrWkzMTx5">DMJX Copenhagen</a
          >
        </p>
        <a
          class={css.register}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noreferrer"
          href="https://forms.gle/oN7YX6pXdhn29YgP7"
        >
          Register
        </a>
        <p class={css.disclaimer}>
          This link opens in a new window as registration for this event is
          handled through Google Forms.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class:list={[grid.container, grid.grid]}>
    <div class:list={[grid.col, css.text]}>
      <h2>Presented in partnership with</h2>
      <ul class={css.partners}>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.dmjx.dk/">
            <img src={dmjx.src} />
          </a>
          <p>Danish School of Media and Journalism</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class:list={[grid.col, css.text]} id="speakers">
      <h2>Speakers</h2>

      <p>More speakers coming soon...</p>

      <p>
        <strong>Lasse Korsgaard</strong>
      </p>

      <img src={lassekorsgaard.src} />

      <p>
        Lasse Korsgaard is a creative developer and interaction designer with
        more than 18 years of experience working at the intersection of design
        and technology. His work ranges from playful experiments with code and
        machine learning to interactive installations and digital experiences.
        He has worked with clients including Google Creative Lab, IKEA and LEGO
        Education and is co-founder of Not a Number, a design-driven development
        studio.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Niels Otto Andersen</strong>
      </p>

      <img src={nielsotto.src} />

      <p>
        Niels Otto graduated in Interactive Design from DMJX and chose to take
        the word “interactivity” in its most literal sense. Through sensors,
        code, lasers, and light, he has created interactive installations,
        visuals, and laser shows, and even helped design Soundboks’ Light Boks.
        Today, Niels Otto works at the restaurant Alchemist and teaches Coded
        Design at DMJX.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Nina-Franziska Christiansen</strong>
      </p>

      <img src={ninafranziskachristiansen.src} />

      <p>
        Nina-Franziska Christiansen is a visual artist and creative technologist
        who uses code and design to build interactive, immersive real-time
        experiences. Her work moves between neuroscience and extended realities,
        from restorative VR to audio-visual performances with collaborators such
        as SØSTR (Sharin Foo and Louise Foo), NEBULAH and Sitrekin (Kirstine
        Stubbe Teglbjærg). She is a researcher at the Royal Danish Academy and
        half of the creative duo Uberørt.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Patrik Hübner</strong>
      </p>

      <img src={patrikhuebner.src} />

      <p>
        Patrik Hübner is an award-winning creative technologist and creative
        director who has worked at the intersection of design and technology
        for over two decades. With deep roots in communication and brand
        design, he has been recognized as a pioneer in combining design, data,
        algorithms, and storytelling to create dynamic branding systems. He
        collaborates with leading brands, agencies, and cultural institutions
        to develop living generative brand systems, and shares his expertise as
        an author, lecturer, keynote speaker, and workshop facilitator.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Paul Nicholas</strong>
      </p>

      <img src={paulnicholas.src} />

      <p>
        Paul is an architect, researcher and educator exploring digital –
        material relations. He is an associate professor at CITA, the Royal
        Danish Academy, and is currently exploring how advanced computation and
        robotic fabrication open up alternative modes for architects to think
        about circularity and sustainability. Paul has an extensive track record
        of developing cutting edge research in biomaterials, robotic incremental
        sheet forming, and predictive modelling methods, and has exhibited at
        the Venice Architecture Biennale in the Arsenale as well as Danish and
        Australian pavilions. As an educator he is the founder and director of
        Computation in Architecture, a 2 year international masters program at
        the Royal Danish Academy that promotes critical and situated thinking
        about technology.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Pernille Lomholt &amp; Eva Petropouleas</strong>
      </p>

      <img src={pernilleeva1.src} />
      <img src={pernilleeva2.src} />

      <p>
        <em>Children creating Algorithmic Art</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        The project ran from 2023-2025, involving children between the age of
        9-14 from 7 different schools. Combining Visual Arts, Mathematics and
        Technological Comprehension led to various local executions and
        different didactical approaches when teaching young beginners coding.
        In the talk Pernille and Eva, both educators at University College
        Absalon, will present some of the highlights and lessons learned
        throughout the project.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class:list={[grid.col, css.text]} id="about">
      <h2>About the Event</h2>
      <p>
        Processing Community Day Copenhagen is a free, one-day event to
        celebrate the intersection of design, art, and code. The day will
        consist of keynotes and lightning talks by people using code for
        creative and experimental projects. It’s a unique chance for everyone
        working with or just interested in code, design and digital art to meet
        in person, share what they're working on, learn from each other, and
        make new connections. The event is open to everyone and requires no
        prior skills or knowledge of coding. Processing Community Day is a
        welcoming place for anyone interested in learning about programming,
        including those not normally represented in the fields of engineering
        and technology.
      </p>
      <p>
        Processing is a free and open-source software platform for learning how
        to code within the context of the visual arts, created by Casey Reas and
        Ben Fry. It is complemented by a web version, called p5.js, created by
        Lauren McCarthy. To date, Processing and p5.js are used by a worldwide
        community of artists, coders, educators and students to create diverse
        art in the fields of graphic design, architecture, dance, photography,
        painting, etc. A focus of the Processing Foundation is to make learning
        how to program and make creative work with code accessible to diverse
        communities, especially those who might not otherwise have access to
        these tools and resources.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class:list={[grid.col, css.text]} id="contact">
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Processing Community Day Copenhagen is organized by{' '}
        <a href="https://andreasrefsgaard.dk/">Andreas Refsgaard</a>,{' '}
        <a href="http://www.stigmollerhansen.dk/">Stig Møller Hansen</a>, and{
          ' '
        }
        <a href="https://runemadsen.com/">Rune Madsen</a>. You can get in touch
        with us on{' '}
        <a href="mailto:organizer@pcdcph.com">organizer@pcdcph.com</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
